  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of insurance policies that John sells on this day.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ customers\n\n- the probability that John succeeds in selling an insurance policy to a given customer is $p=0.45$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\u223cB(8,0.45).$ We want to find the probability that he will sell exactly $2$ insurance policies.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2})(0.45)^{2}(1-0.45)^{8-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8!}{2!(8-2)!}(0.45)^{2}(0.55)^{6} \\\\  & =28(0.45)^{2}(0.55)^{6} \\\\  & \u22480.157\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
